  "blog.beginner.advanced.desc": "For more control over your pixel art, explore the advanced settings:",
  "blog.beginner.advanced.dithering": "<strong>Enable Floyd-Steinberg Dithering:</strong> This technique helps to smooth out color transitions and reduce banding, especially in images with limited color palettes.",
  "blog.beginner.advanced.grid": "<strong>Show Pixel Grid:</strong> Overlay a grid on your preview to visualize individual pixels, useful for precise placement on the Wplace canvas.",
  "blog.beginner.advanced.scaling": "<strong>Image Scaling Method:</strong> Choose between Nearest Neighbor (sharpest, blocky), Bilinear (smoother, slightly blurry), and Lanczos (high-quality, good for downscaling).",
  "blog.beginner.advanced.title": "3. Advanced Settings (Optional)",
  "blog.beginner.closing": "Now that you know the basics of the Wplace Paint Tool, it's time to start creating your own pixel art! Upload your first image, adjust the settings, and admire the results. As you gain experience, you can experiment with more complex images and techniques. Happy creating on Wplace!",
  "blog.beginner.download.desc": "Once you're satisfied with your pixel art, you can download it directly from the preview area. You'll have options to download with or without the pixel grid, depending on your needs for placement on the Wplace canvas.",
  "blog.beginner.download.title": "5. Downloading Your Pixel Art",
  "blog.beginner.intro": "Welcome to the Wplace Paint Tool! This guide will walk you through everything you need to know to transform your images into stunning pixel art for the Wplace canvas. Whether you're a seasoned pixel artist or just starting, our tool makes the process simple and fun.",
  "blog.beginner.palette.desc": "Our tool automatically converts your image to the official Wplace 64-color palette, ensuring perfect compatibility with the platform. The preview area will show you exactly how your artwork will appear on the canvas, along with a breakdown of the colors used.",
  "blog.beginner.palette.title": "4. Understanding the Wplace Color Palette",
  "blog.beginner.pixel_size.desc": "The pixel size slider is your primary control for defining the level of detail in your pixel art. A smaller pixel size will result in more detail and a larger image, while a larger pixel size will create a more abstract, blocky appearance.",
  "blog.beginner.pixel_size.experiment": "Experiment with different values to find the perfect balance for your artwork.",
